From: claude <claude@p7x.dev>
Subject: [freebsd-ports PATCH v7] net-mgmt/signoz: Observability platform for
 logs, metrics, and traces
To: Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com>
Cc: lab-devel@p7x.dev
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 04:10:26 +0000

SigNoz is an open source observability platform. It brings logs,
metrics, and traces together in one application, with dashboards,
alerts, exceptions, and service maps. It is native to OpenTelemetry and
stores its data in ClickHouse.

This is the Community Edition of the query service and web interface.
Telemetry reaches ClickHouse through the SigNoz distribution of the
OpenTelemetry Collector, which is packaged separately.

Upstream pins github.com/bytedance/sonic v1.14.1, which does not build
with Go 1.26 or later. The port vendors sonic from an upstream commit
after v1.15.4 instead; that commit also fixes mmap(2) on FreeBSD, which
no sonic release includes yet.

WWW: https://signoz.io/
---
Notes for review:

Build: on the base commit below, with Go 1.27.2 and STRICT_DEPENDS
set, makesum fetched into an empty DISTDIR with the network up. Then,
as in a poudriere jail, DISTDIR was made read-only and the network was
cut by pointing the proxy variables at a closed port. extract, patch,
build, stage, and check-plist all passed, and go logged no download
after fetch. The staged binary, run as an unprivileged user with no
ClickHouse, started its HTTP servers and ran for 30 seconds without a
panic. portclippy, portfmt, and portlint are clean. Not checked: a
real poudriere build, aarch64, and a run from rc.d (that needs root).

Why sonic must be replaced: signoz v0.145.0 pins
github.com/bytedance/sonic v1.14.1. Its internal/rt package defines
GoMapIterator only for go1.24 && !go1.26, so with the tree's Go 1.26
or 1.27 it fails with "undefined: GoMapIterator". This is not specific
to FreeBSD, and go.mk offers no older Go. sonic added Go 1.26 support
in v1.15.0. signoz main and v0.146.0 still pin v1.14.1; SigNoz PR
#11975, "fix(deps): bump bytedance/sonic to v1.15.0 for Go 1.26
compatibility", has been open since July. Separately, sonic's loader
maps memory with MAP_ANON and fd 0, which FreeBSD rejects with EINVAL,
so the binary panics at startup. bytedance/sonic commit bf12ab8,
"MAP_ANON should be set to -1 , not required on Linux, required on
BSD. + go fmt", fixes that and is in no release yet.

How, and the prior art: go.mk vendors the modules at extract time, in
go-post-extract, so a patch to go.mod in files/ comes too late. The
Porter's Handbook says so in "Overriding go.mod in Modules Mode"
(section 6.6.8). The tree's common answer, for a GO_MODULE port that
needs a newer copy of one module, is to fetch it with GH_TUPLE and
swap it into vendor/ in pre-patch. 22 GO_MODULE ports do this, e.g.
security/nebula (x/net, x/term, x/sys), databases/imposm3,
net/slackcat, and www/oneshot. This version follows nebula: one
GH_TUPLE entry and a two-line pre-patch.

The tuple is commit bf12ab8 rather than a tag, since only that commit
has the mmap fix. It is v1.15.4 plus five bug fixes, and its loader
differs from loader/v0.5.2 only in mmap_unix.go. sonic v1.15.4 needs
no new modules: its go.mod differs from v1.14.1's only in the loader
version. go.mod and vendor/modules.txt still name v1.14.1 and v0.3.0,
so go's vendor consistency check passes. The cost is that "go version
-m" reports those versions, as it does for nebula's x/ modules. When a
sonic release has the fix, the tuple becomes that tag.

This drops post-fetch, post-extract, SONIC_EDIT, the six proxy lines,
PATCH_SITES, PATCHFILES, and PATCH_WRKSRC. Only www/comentario
downloads an extra module in its own post-fetch, so it was a poor
model. The mmap fix still comes from upstream's GitHub through the
framework, now as the commit's tree. No port in the tree applies
PATCHFILES to vendored code. Two alternatives were rejected:
- Override go.mod and go.sum as distfiles, as the handbook's example
  does for textproc/miller. The edited go.mod would need hosting
  (LOCAL on freefall), and the loader would still need its own fix.
- Add files/patch-go.mod, patch-go.sum and patch-vendor_modules.txt
  to the swap, as net/slackcat and databases/cayley do. That is three
  more patches whose only effect is accurate version metadata.

The other workarounds, each with the ports that do the same:
- :web, the prebuilt frontend from upstream's linux_amd64 release
  tarball. www/grafana installs public/ from its linux-amd64 tarball
  the same way, and net-mgmt/prometheus3 and net-mgmt/alertmanager
  fetch a prebuilt web UI release asset. The files are not
  architecture-specific, so the amd64 tarball serves aarch64 too.
  Source maps, 31 MB of the 54 MB, are left out because only browser
  developer tools read them. v6 said the pnpm build has no offline
  path in the tree. That was wrong: Mk/Uses/npm.mk prefetches
  node_modules as a distfile, and www/remark42 builds a pnpm frontend
  with USES=npm:pnpm. Building would add Node and a node_modules
  distfile to every build. I recommend the prebuilt files, as grafana
  does.
- post-patch REINPLACE_CMD for default paths. About 55 Go ports use
  it; it is the most common way. irc/soju rewrites /var/lib/soju to
  /var/db/soju, and security/nebula rewrites /etc/nebula. ldflags -X,
  as security/crowdsec uses, sets only package variables, and
  signoz's defaults are struct literals in its config constructors.
  The active query log path is one of them: it defaults to "", the
  working directory, which is / under rc.d.
- post-install appends the web files to TMPPLIST with find. www/grafana
  does the same for its public/ tree. The file names carry content
  hashes that change with every release.
- USERS and GROUPS (UID 485) and ONLY_FOR_ARCHS are unchanged from
  v6.

Changes in v7:
- Vendor sonic from a GH_TUPLE commit swapped in pre-patch, following
  security/nebula, instead of a hand-written post-fetch download and
  go mod edit. The commit includes the mmap fix, so the PATCHFILES
  entry and PATCH_WRKSRC are gone.
- Name the replaced sonic pin in the commit message.
- Link to v6: id:65e5d96216421fede90ada8a07333f559d92b029.1792028652.git.claude@p7x.dev

